    "two_pct_depth_unresponsive_note": null,
    "two_pct_depth_basis": "A FLOOR, not a measurement. The deepest rung quoted ($2,000,000) still clears inside 200bps, so the crossing is somewhere above where we look. Publishing it as '2% depth' would read as a limit we found. SLIPPAGE CONVENTION: TOTAL over the fill (not marginal impact at the margin), measured against this venue's own smallest quoted rung. \u26a0\ufe0f That reference EXCLUDES the smallest rung's own spread, so the figure is price impact and NOT the holder's all-in cost \u2014 which is fill_ratio (output_usd / size). Both are real and they answer different questions. The same ladder classifies differently under total vs marginal and under impact vs all-in, so both halves are stated for cross-producer comparison.",
    "total_2pct_depth_note": "\u26a0\ufe0f A FLOOR now, not a null and never TVL. The note here used to read 'no executable ladder is quoted for this asset' -- stale: a KyberSwap ladder IS quoted, clears $500K, and re-quoting live reaches $2M at -2.65bps. The GeckoTerminal-venue objection below is about POOL ENUMERATION and does not reach an aggregator quote, which routes through Fluid like anything else. \u26a0\ufe0f This field previously republished POOL TVL ($3,287,675.12) as depth \u2014 the same value to the cent under both keys \u2014 and the Liquidity axis was rated off it. TO ESTABLISH IT: a quote ladder on the venue USDai actually routes through, which is Fluid and not the GeckoTerminal pairs; that venue set is wrong for depth exactly as it is already documented to be for volume.",
    "total_tvl_basis": "POOL TVL, NOT A MEASURED LADDER. Summed GeckoTerminal reserve of the USDai/USDC pairs, republished under the depth field because no executable ladder is quoted. \u26a0\ufe0f It OVERSTATES executable depth: a one-sided pool can hold this much and still cost hundreds of bps to exit at a fraction of it. TO ESTABLISH REAL DEPTH: a quote ladder on the venue USDai actually routes through \u2014 Fluid, not these pairs, so the venue set is wrong for depth as it is for volume.",
